<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for the hardened-runtime macOS build.

  We intentionally do NOT enable the App Sandbox — the helper needs to
  read/write user-owned MT5 data directories under
  `~/Library/Application Support/**/MQL5/Experts/` that are outside
  any sandbox container path. The hardened runtime (sans sandbox)
  plus notarization satisfies Gatekeeper without boxing us in.

  allow-jit + allow-unsigned-executable-memory are both required by
  WebKit's JS engine (WebKit.framework embeds its JIT and reaches for
  unsigned memory pages for bytecode). Omitting either crashes the
  webview on first navigation inside a hardened-runtime build.

  allow-dyld-environment-variables is required so Tauri's own runtime
  can set DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH / DYLD_FRAMEWORK_PATH during startup — a
  no-op on notarized production builds but load-bearing during local
  development when running the unsigned debug binary.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
  <true/>
  <!--
    NOTE: We tried adding `keychain-access-groups` here in v0.0.6 to
    pin the data-protection keychain group to a stable value across
    builds. AMFI rejected the launch with -413 "No matching profile
    found" because keychain-access-groups is a *restricted*
    entitlement that requires an embedded provisioning profile —
    something Developer ID (non-App-Store) distribution doesn't have.

    For Developer ID apps, the implicit access group
    (<TeamID>.<bundleid>) is granted automatically as long as the
    app's signing identity stays stable. The keyring crate's modern
    SecItem-based path uses this implicit group on macOS, and entries
    persist correctly across launches of the same signed binary.

    If we ever need an explicit group (e.g. for sharing keychain
    items between sibling helper apps under the same team), we'd
    need to enroll a Mac App Store / TestFlight distribution path
    with proper provisioning profiles. Not worth it.
  -->
</dict>
</plist>
